From: stephan-xyz <stephan@zehrer.xyz>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 17:00:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] redox: timer thread blocks all signals

timer_settime starts a thread that sends SIGEV_SIGNAL signals to the
process. The thread inherited the caller's signal mask, so it could take
the signal itself; a thread waiting for it in sem_wait or pause was then
not interrupted (tests/time/timer: the second sem_wait got EINVAL, or
the first returned 0). glibc blocks all signals in its timer thread too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/platform/redox/timer.rs | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/platform/redox/timer.rs b/src/platform/redox/timer.rs
index 5e9f557..0580bb6 100644
--- a/src/platform/redox/timer.rs
+++ b/src/platform/redox/timer.rs
@@ -75,6 +75,12 @@ impl RlctTimer {
 }
 
 pub extern "C" fn timer_routine(arg: *mut c_void) -> *mut c_void {
+    // SIGEV_SIGNAL sends the signal to the process, so any thread that does
+    // not block it may take it, this one too. Then the thread that waits for
+    // it (sem_wait, pause, ...) is not interrupted: it sees at most what the
+    // handler did, on another thread. Block all signals here, as glibc does
+    // in its timer thread.
+    let _ = redox_rt::signal::set_sigmask(Some(!0), None);
     let (mut timer_version, eventfd) = {
         let timers = TIMERS.lock();
         let Some(timer_st) = timers.get(&arg.addr()) else {
